WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.940
A deleted final showdown for Black Widow,
and more Red Guardian, Taskmaster, and Yelena

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.820
moments were all cut from the final version
of the movie.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.000
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video, I'm revealing all the deleted

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.090
scenes from Black Widow, a post-credits moment
that teased Natasha's return from the dead,

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.660
plus the Avenger cameo you never got to see!

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.200
Spoilers ahead, of course, so take care.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.950
The Black Widow post-credits scene sees Yelena
visiting Natasha's tombstone in Ohio and as

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.270
she mourns her sister she whistles softly.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.460
(WHISTLES LONG NOTE)
This is the same whistle the sisters used

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:37.360
to call to each other as children and which
they also did as they parted at the end of

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.360
the movie.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.390
(WHISTLES LONG NOTE)
(WHISTLES LONG NOTE)

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.910
In the end-credits scene, after Yelena whistles
there's a brief pause as if she waits or hopes

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:48.870
for a response, as do we, but there isn't
one.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:53.400
However, Black Widow director Cate Shortland
has now revealed in an interview with Variety

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.060
that there were discussions about including
a whistle reply in the scene.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:01.580
Natasha, of course, died in Endgame when she
gave her life in return for the Soul Stone

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:05.580
and, together with Tony Stark, she's one of
the few Avengers that have so far remained

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.290
dead in the MCU.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.490
If Yelena had heard a reply to her whistle,
even if it seemed like it wasn't real and

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.990
she'd imagined it, then I expect it would
have fuelled lots of speculation that Natasha

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:21.360
could soon return in an upcoming MCU project,
similar to some fans' wishful hoping about

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.229
Iron Man's hammer sound at the end of Endgame.

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:29.210
Many other dead Marvel characters have already
made a return to the MCU including Vision

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.320
and Loki, albeit in different versions or
timelines,

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.830
and even Coulson was reanimated for Agents
of SHIELD after Loki killed him in The Avengers,

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:41.119
so I wouldn't be totally opposed to the OG
Black Widow returning if it was done in the

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:42.340
right way.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.650
After all, we don't know exactly what happens
to a character when they go the MCU version

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.810
of the Soul World and whether there's any
way they could be retrieved.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:54.590
Thanos saw and spoke to a young version of
dead Gamora after he snapped, a hint that

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:56.729
the Soul World could be accessible perhaps.

00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:01.100
Of course, there'd be many fans who'd love
for Natasha to return in a Black Widow sequel

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.530
or even a different movie,
and I think that including the whistle response

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.420
would have given fans the impression that
Marvel was committed to doing that in some

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.530
way, which is my guess for why they ultimately
didn't do it.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.989
Leave a thumbs-up if you wish there'd been
a whistle response and let me know whether

00:02:14.989 --> 00:02:17.900
you think Natasha should come back from the
dead.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.510
The main events of the movie culminate with
Team Natasha taking out Dreykov, liberating

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.680
his brainwashed widows, and crashing the airborne
Red Room to the ground.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:32.060
As Secretary Ross together with a large military
convoy heads towards the crash site, Nat sends

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:37.400
her adoptive family and the Red Room survivors
away, while she waits to face the music alone.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:42.730
What exactly happens next is left to our imagination
as the film cuts to black before returning

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:47.079
with an epilogue scene set two weeks later
that shows Natasha is still free.

00:02:47.079 --> 00:02:51.719
Footage from the Black Widow trailers, however,
indicates that originally there was an alternate

00:02:51.719 --> 00:02:56.040
or extended version of this ending where Ross
gets out to survey the scene together with

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.290
his SWAT team from the beginning of the movie.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:02.469
You can see from the Hungarian writing on
the truck behind Ross that the Red Room appeared

00:03:02.469 --> 00:03:06.300
to crash down somewhere in Hungary, perhaps
even near Budapest.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.890
There's also deleted footage in the trailers
of armed marksmen emerging from cars and scores

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:15.010
of military personnel taking up positions
among the wreckage of the Red Room with their

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.480
sights presumably set on Natasha.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.659
It seems that this would have been an almost
impossible situation for Natasha to escape

00:03:21.659 --> 00:03:25.590
from by herself although given some of the
crazy fun action in the movie,

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:30.019
it's not unfeasible to think she might have
found a way out, given she and Hawkeye survived

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:33.549
a shootout with the Hungarian Special Forces
many years earlier.

00:03:33.549 --> 00:03:38.279
It's also possible that she somehow struck
a deal with Ross or tricked him again in some

00:03:38.279 --> 00:03:42.139
way though I don't think he'd be especially
amenable to a negotiation.

00:03:42.139 --> 00:03:46.760
There were some great moments with David Harbour's
Red Guardian while he's locked up in the Russian

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.799
prison and we find him bragging about the
supposed battles he had with Captain America!

00:03:51.799 --> 00:03:53.139
What year was this?

00:03:53.139 --> 00:03:54.819
Like, '83, '84.

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:57.720
Captain America was still frozen in ice then.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:02.099
And the prison break when Natasha and Yelena
come to rescue him originally included more

00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:04.709
extensive Red Guardian combat scenes.

00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:08.531
The marketing materials show him fighting
other prisoners who got out after a prison

00:04:08.531 --> 00:04:10.040
riot kicked off.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.189
And in some behind-the-scenes footage, we
can also see Alexei fighting off a pack of

00:04:14.189 --> 00:04:18.750
prison guards, whereas in the final version
Red Guardian's only encounter with a guard

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.900
is while he's climbing up one of the walls.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:25.600
I suspect the longer prison fight was cut
for time given the final movie was already

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.970
around two hours and a quarter, though personally
I never felt the film dragged.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:34.500
Some more Red Guardian moments from the trailers
that didn't make it into the final movie,

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.470
include Alexei flexing and admiring his muscles
in Melina's bathroom which is deleted from

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.100
the scene where he decides to put on his old
Red Guardian suit.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:47.590
We also don't get to see him putting on his
helmet as he does here in the promos.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:51.790
And Alexei's reaction after he leaves the
bathroom with his costume on is noticeably

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:56.810
different in the trailer compared to the final
movie.

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:02.270
Still fits!

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:03.280
Still fits.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.790
Speaking to Inverse, director Cate Shortland
said that there was a good deal of improvisation

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:10.480
on set, especially during the dinner table
reunion.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.350
And I found some more dialogue by Alexei for
that scene in the trailers that wasn't used

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:15.350
in the final film.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:16.360
Back together again.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:17.430
You got fat.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:18.850
It's mainly water weight.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:22.390
In the final version of the movie, this is
how that exchange goes instead:

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:23.420
You got fat.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:24.450
But still good.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.510
I just got out of prison.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:27.510
I, uh...

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.190
I have a lot of energy.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:30.190
Oh!

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:31.190
Please don't do that.

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:35.280
Also in the trailers, but not in the final
film, is footage of Taskmaster watching Black

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.480
Widow fight and analysing her moves.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:39.030
His call sign's Taskmaster.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:42.740
And that line from Yelena did not make it
into the final film either.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:46.840
By the way, the footage from that cut scene
is actually taken from Natasha's hallway fight

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.310
in Iron Man 2.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:53.430
In the final movie, Taskmaster analyses Natasha's
moves during the fight on the bridge rather

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:58.770
than in advance and, instead, we see the antagonist
studying a Hawkeye fight scene, which sets

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:02.500
up the Taskmaster arrow attack on Nat and
Yelena in Budapest.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:07.000
There's also a small piece of dialogue that
Dreykov appears to say to Taskmaster in the

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.040
trailers that wasn't in the final film.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.960
Bring her...
home.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.720
This appears to imply that originally part
of Taskmaster's mission was to capture Natasha

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:20.250
or Yelena and take her to the Red Room, rather
than just retrieve the antidote vials which

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.610
seemed to be her main mission in the finished
film.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:27.510
The story of who was underneath the mask and
how she was part of Natasha's red ledger fitted

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:31.490
well with the overall theme of the movie about
the abuse of girls and women.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:36.660
However, I think the movie's promotional material
led many fans to believe Taskmaster was going

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:41.170
to be a much bigger villain, for example,
the way one trailer was cut even implied that

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.810
Taskmaster was going to be the big bad rather
than Dreykov.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:46.380
His call sign's Taskmaster.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:47.680
He controls the Red Room.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:51.010
I really enjoyed the film;
however, the marketing may well have set the

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:56.290
wrong expectations for fans going in to the
movie when it came to Taskmaster's role.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:07:01.350
Natasha and Yelena also had some deleted and
alternate versions of their scenes in Budapest.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:05.280
After their fight in the apartment, this quip
from Natasha was cut from the final movie.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.450
It's good to see you too sis.

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:11.150
And then there's a line from Yelena that appeared
in lot of the promos for the film.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:15.170
Natasha my sister, after all this time, what
brings you home?

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:19.680
I can see why this was cut as it doesn't make
a lot of sense in the movie given that it

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.630
was Yelena who sent Natasha the vials of antidote
together with their childhood photo in the

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:27.060
first place, so Yelena already knew why Natasha
had come home.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.490
And there's also a line deleted from Yelena's
dialogue about what she imagined Natasha's

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:32.990
life would have been like in America.

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:37.120
You're thinking about moving, but you're waiting
for the interest rates to go down.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.690
We did get to briefly see some of the Red
Room widows training in unison after Team

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.960
Natasha arrived on the floating platform.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:48.900
However, as you might expect for any big film
like this and as we can see from the trailers,

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.560
a lot more footage was shot of the Widows'
training choreography that didn't make it

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:54.190
into the final cut.

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:59.910
And a cameo by Tony Stark was also apparently
on the cards according to a September 2019

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:03.310
Deadline report from the Saturn Awards where
it was stated that,

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:07.800
"Downey will be seen in the role of Stark
one more time in the Marvel prequel Black

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:08.800
Widow."

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.300
Clearly Tony Stark wasn't in the movie, although
Yelena did mention him at one point as one

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:17.820
of Natasha's Avenger scientist friends who
she thought could help analyse the mind-control

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:18.820
antidote formula.

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:21.770
Why don't you take it to one of your super-scientist
friends?

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:23.110
Tony Stark, maybe?

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:26.990
Given their final words in Civil War though,
I doubt they'd have been on speaking terms

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:30.300
during the main events of the Black Widow,
so I don't think it would have made a lot

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.130
of sense for him to appear in the film.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:34.749
T'Challa told Ross what you did, so...
they're coming for you.

00:08:34.749 --> 00:08:37.269
I'm not the one that needs to watch their
back.

00:08:37.269 --> 00:08:42.249
Still, possible scenarios where he might have
fit would have been via a flashback or reusing

00:08:42.249 --> 00:08:47.259
footage from a previous movie, like when Thor:
Ragnarok used an Age of Ultron clip of Natasha

00:08:47.259 --> 00:08:49.649
to calm the Hulk and bring back Banner.

00:08:49.649 --> 00:08:54.019
Alternately, Tony could have been a voice
over the phone, similar to how Hawkeye's voice

00:08:54.019 --> 00:08:57.850
was in the movie during the flashback to the
assassination attempt on Dreykov.

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:00.240
BARTON: (ON RADIO) We need confirmation Dreykov's
in the building.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.269
Since recording this video, Cate Shortland
has revealed in an interview with Games Radar

00:09:04.269 --> 00:09:08.540
that initially there were discussions about
whether any of the other Avengers or existing

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:13.309
Marvel characters should be included in the
movie, but in the end both she and Kevin Feige

00:09:13.309 --> 00:09:16.000
agreed that,
"She doesn't need the boys.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.250
We didn't want it to feel like she needs the
support.

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:20.050
We want her to stand alone.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:21.069
And she does."

